
  [38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m matches what [38;5;153msrc/[39m already uses, so this isn't a new pattern in the repo — just one applied on both
  sides of a file.

  [1mRunner:[0m Node's built-in [38;5;153mnode:test[39m with [38;5;153mnode --test[39m, wired as [38;5;153mnpm test[39m in [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m. That keeps the repo's
  zero-dependency property intact, which felt worth preserving given there isn't so much as a lockfile today.

  [1mWhat gets tested[0m — the branches, not the happy path alone: defaults on empty storage; set/get round-trip and the exact
  bytes landing in storage; unparseable JSON, a non-object payload, and an unrecognised [38;5;153mversion[39m each degrading to
  all-defaults; one field failing validation while its neighbours survive; a storage key absent from the schema being
  dropped and not written back; unknown-key and invalid-value calls throwing; [38;5;153msetItem[39m throwing leaving the in-memory
  value updated without propagating; storage access throwing at construction falling back to memory; and [38;5;153mreset[39m/[38;5;153mclear[39m.

  The [38;5;153mapp.js[39m wiring — prefill, the checkbox, save-on-success — is DOM-coupled and stays manually verified. Adding jsdom
  to test three lines of glue isn't a trade I'd make here, but say so if you'd rather have it covered.

  Last thing, and then I'll write the spec. This repo has no tooling configured at all — no linter, no formatter, no
  test runner. Setting any of it up is cheapest right now, before there's code to retrofit, and whatever you pick
  becomes a standing constraint for this work.
